'use strict';
// Static map features for the in-app quest maps: extracts, hazards, keyed doors,
// fixed loose-loot spawn points and marked rooms.
//
// GENERATED from tarkov.dev's GraphQL API — `node _dev/build_mapmarkers.js`.
// Don't hand-edit: regenerate, and diff. Coordinates are game (x, y, z) and go
// through the same mapPoint()/floorOf() in mapdata.js that the quest pins use,
// so anything wrong here is wrong the same way for both.
//
// ── ON "GUARANTEED" SPAWNS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// There is NO spawn chance, weight or "guaranteed" flag anywhere in Tarkov's
// public data. `lootLoose` gives a position and the list of items that CAN
// appear there — nothing more. So this file cannot, and does not, claim any item
// always spawns anywhere.
//
// What the data does support is EXCLUSIVITY: how many other items compete for
// the same exact spot. `alts` is that number.
//   alts === 0  -> nothing else can spawn at that point (the strongest claim
//                  the data allows, and the closest honest thing to "reliable")
//   alts 1..4   -> a fixed spot shared with 1 to 4 other items
// Positions listing 6 or more possible items are dropped: at that dilution a marker says
// little beyond "loot exists here". The marked rooms (`mk`) are kept separately
// precisely because they are the opposite case — dozens to hundreds of items in
// one pool — and their pool size is carried so the UI can say so out loud.
//
// Worked example, and the reason this warning exists: the Machinery Key has
// exactly ONE loose position on Customs, and that position lists 131 possible
// items — it is the Dorms marked room. "The machinery key always spawns in
// dorms" is therefore false as stated; the marked room is where it *can* roll.
//
// Row layouts (see MARKER_COLS — read it, don't hard-code indices):
//   ex  [x, y, z, faction, needsSwitch, name, tollItem, tollCount]
//                                                   faction 0=pmc 1=scav 2=shared
//                                                   toll = what the extract charges
//                                                   ("RUB" 20000, "Code", "Mines"), "" if free
//   hz  [x, y, z, type]                              type    0=minefield 1=sniper 2=other
//   lk  [x, y, z, type, keyShort, keyName]           type    0=door 1=trunk 2=container 3=switch
//   lt  — NO LONGER IN THIS FILE. The loose-loot layer rows are rebuilt by the
//        app from LT_POS/LT_ITEMS/LT_CATS below (see lootRowsFor in
//        renderer.js), and MARKER_COLS still describes the shape it rebuilds:
//        [x, y, z, cat, alts, itemName]. They were dropped because they answer
//        only half the question: a row keeps the DEAREST item of each category
//        at a point, so "where does BakeEzy spawn" came back as 3 spots on one
//        map when the real answer is 148 across seven. Deriving them costs
//        nothing — the file got smaller.
//   mk  [x, y, z, poolSize, keysInPool]              keysInPool = key names in that pool, "" if none
//   co  [x, y, z, type]                              type = index into CONTAINER_TYPES
//   tr  [x, y, z, desc, dest]                        transit to another map; dest = map name.
//                                                     Sourced from json.tarkov.dev (GraphQL
//                                                     never carried them here).
//   sw  [x, y, z, what, opens]                       a switch / lever. `what` is a human
//                                                     sentence resolved at bake time ("Opens
//                                                     the D-2 extract · Needs another switch
//                                                     thrown first") — the raw data only has
//                                                     internal ids. `opens` names the EXTRACT(s)
//                                                     it unlocks ("|"-joined, "" if none), which
//                                                     is what draws the switch->extract line.
//                                                     Also json.tarkov.dev.
//
// LOOT_VALUE prices every lt item: name -> [rouble value, value per slot], from
// json.tarkov.dev at bake time — max(flea avg24h, flea last-low, best trader
// sell) across both modes. LOOT_HV_MIN is the bar the renderer's "high value"
// layer applies to the ITEM'S OWN WORTH (value[0]); the per-slot figure is
// carried for the card, not for the test. Data-driven by design: whatever
// clears the bar is flagged, never a hand list of item names.
//
// A static container is part of the level geometry, so it is in that spot every
// raid — only its CONTENTS are a roll. Worth saying on the card; NOT a title,
// because "always here" reads as a promise of loot and it is not one.
// `co` carries one code per REAL container type, not a family bucket, so a
// toolbox and a technical supply crate stay tellable apart.
//
// Hazards carry no polygon outline upstream (every `outline` is empty), so they
// are points. The dense clouds — Lighthouse 344, Streets 197 — trace the mined
// borders on their own; don't write code expecting polygons.
//
// Map feature data: tarkov.dev (the-hideout), MIT. Full notices in
// THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt.

